  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":61,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A market trader saves 15 000 FCFA in the first month. In each following month she saves 12 000 FCFA more than the month before. Write the amount saved in month n as a formula.\\n\\nb In which month does she save more than 100 000 FCFA for the first time? Show that the total saved up to that month is more than 500 000 FCFA.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A market trader saves 15 000 FCFA in the first month. In each following month she saves 12 000 FCFA more than the month before. Write the amount saved in month n as a formula."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b In which month does she save more than 100 000 FCFA for the first time? Show that the total saved up to that month is more than 500 000 FCFA."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Arithmetic sequence: a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000 → aₙ = 15 000 + (n − 1) × 12 000 = 12 000n + 3000. (b) 12 000n + 3000 > 100 000 → 12 000n > 97 000 → n > 8.08 → the first month is month 9 (saving 12 000 × 9 + 3000 = 111 000 FCFA). Total up to month 9: S₉ = 9 ÷ 2 × (2 × 15 000 + 8 × 12 000) = 4.5 × (30 000 + 96 000) = 4.5 × 126 000 = 567 000 FCFA — more than 500 000 ✓. So by month 9 she both saves more than 100 000 in a month and has accumulated more than 500 000 in total.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000, 1 for the formula (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for month 9 (from the inequality), 1 for S₉ = 567 000 with the sum formula shown, 1 for a clear conclusion linking both thresholds.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — unsimplified formula forms; month 9; 567 000 FCFA.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":61,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Formula first, then an inequality, then the sum of nine months.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) amount saved in month n","prompt":"Write the amount saved in month n as a formula.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["Arithmetic: a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000.","Write a₁ + (n − 1)d, then expand and collect the like terms."],"accepted":["aₙ = 12 000n + 3000","an = 12000n + 3000","12000n+3000","aₙ = 15 000 + 12 000(n − 1)","15000 + 12000(n-1)","uₙ = 12 000n + 3000"]},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) first month over 100 000 FCFA","prompt":"In which month does she save more than 100 000 FCFA for the first time?","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Solve 12 000n + 3000 > 100 000.","Take the smallest whole month above the threshold."],"answer":9,"tolerance":0,"unit":"month"},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) total saved up to that month","prompt":"Show that the total saved up to that month is more than 500 000 FCFA.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × (2a₁ + (n − 1)d) with n = 9, a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000.","The bracket is 2a₁ + 8d — work it out, multiply by n ÷ 2, then compare with 500 000."],"answer":567000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"FCFA"},{"id":"b3","label":"(b) conclusion","prompt":"State your conclusion: does she reach the target by month 9, and why?","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Evaluate your nth term at the month from part (b1) and compare with 100 000.","Evaluate your sum at that same month and compare with 500 000."],"accepted":["yes — in month 9 she saves 111 000 (>100 000) and the total reaches 567 000 (>500 000)","month 9: 111 000 saved that month; 567 000 in total, both above target"],"keywords":[["month 9","9th","ninth","9"],["111 000","111,000","111000"],["567 000","567,000","567000"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Arithmetic sequence: a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000 → aₙ = 15 000 + (n − 1) × 12 000 = 12 000n + 3000. (b) 12 000n + 3000 > 100 000 → 12 000n > 97 000 → n > 8.08 → the first month is month 9 (saving 12 000 × 9 + 3000 = 111 000 FCFA). Total up to month 9: S₉ = 9 ÷ 2 × (2 × 15 000 + 8 × 12 000) = 4.5 × (30 000 + 96 000) = 4.5 × 126 000 = 567 000 FCFA — more than 500 000 ✓. So by month 9 she both saves more than 100 000 in a month and has accumulated more than 500 000 in total.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000, 1 for the formula (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for month 9 (from the inequality), 1 for S₉ = 567 000 with the sum formula shown, 1 for a clear conclusion linking both thresholds.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — unsimplified formula forms; month 9; 567 000 FCFA.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Arithmetic sequence: a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000 → aₙ = 15 000 + (n − 1) × 12 000 = 12 000n + 3000. (b) 12 000n + 3000 > 100 000 → 12 000n > 97 000 → n > 8.08 → the first month is month 9 (saving 12 000 × 9 + 3000 = 111 000 FCFA). Total up to month 9: S₉ = 9 ÷ 2 × (2 × 15 000 + 8 × 12 000) = 4.5 × (30 000 + 96 000) = 4.5 × 126 000 = 567 000 FCFA — more than 500 000 ✓. So by month 9 she both saves more than 100 000 in a month and has accumulated more than 500 000 in total.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000, 1 for the formula (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for month 9 (from the inequality), 1 for S₉ = 567 000 with the sum formula shown, 1 for a clear conclusion linking both thresholds.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — unsimplified formula forms; month 9; 567 000 FCFA.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Arithmetic sequence: a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000 → aₙ = 15 000 + (n − 1) × 12 000 = 12 000n + 3000. (b) 12 000n + 3000 > 100 000 → 12 000n > 97 000 → n > 8.08 → the first month is month 9 (saving 12 000 × 9 + 3000 = 111 000 FCFA). Total up to month 9: S₉ = 9 ÷ 2 × (2 × 15 000 + 8 × 12 000) = 4.5 × (30 000 + 96 000) = 4.5 × 126 000 = 567 000 FCFA — more than 500 000 ✓. So by month 9 she both saves more than 100 000 in a month and has accumulated more than 500 000 in total.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for identifying a₁ = 15 000, d = 12 000, 1 for the formula (either form). (b) 3 marks: 1 for month 9 (from the inequality), 1 for S₉ = 567 000 with the sum formula shown, 1 for a clear conclusion linking both thresholds.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — unsimplified formula forms; month 9; 567 000 FCFA."},{"n":62,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Three consecutive terms of an arithmetic sequence are k + 1, 2k + 1 and 5k − 5, in that order. Find the value of k.\\n\\nb Write down the three terms and the common difference, and check that the middle term is the average of the other two.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three consecutive terms of an arithmetic sequence are k + 1, 2k + 1 and 5k − 5, in that order. Find the value of k."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Write down the three terms and the common difference, and check that the middle term is the average of the other two."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) For three consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence the middle is the average of the outer two: 2(2k + 1) = (k + 1) + (5k − 5) → 4k + 2 = 6k − 4 → 6 = 2k → k = 3. Equivalently, 2k + 1 − (k + 1) = 5k − 5 − (2k + 1) → k = 3. (b) The terms are 4, 7, 10 and d = 3. Check: (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 — the middle term is indeed the average of the other two ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the correct equation (average property or equal differences), 1 for correct algebra/simplification, 1 for k = 3. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three terms 4, 7, 10 and d = 3, 1 for the average check (4 + 10)/2 = 7.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — working via either equation; ‘k = 3’; check any valid form.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":62,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"The middle term of an arithmetic triple is the average of the other two.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) the value of k","prompt":"Find the value of k.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["In an arithmetic sequence the middle term is the average of the other two: 2(2k + 1) = (k + 1) + (5k − 5).","Solve the simplified equation for k."],"answer":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) the three terms and d","prompt":"Write down the three terms and the common difference.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Substitute your own k into the three terms.","Subtract consecutive terms to get d."],"accepted":["4, 7, 10; d = 3","4,7,10; common difference 3","terms 4, 7, 10 with d = 3"],"keywords":[["4"],["7"],["10"],["3"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) middle-term check","prompt":"Check that the middle term is the average of the other two.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["(4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 = middle term ✓.","The property holds — as expected for an arithmetic sequence."],"accepted":["(4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7, which is the middle term — correct","average of 4 and 10 is 7, equal to the middle term ✓","7 = (4 + 10)/2 ✓"],"keywords":[["7"],["(4 + 10)","average","mean","(4+10)"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) For three consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence the middle is the average of the outer two: 2(2k + 1) = (k + 1) + (5k − 5) → 4k + 2 = 6k − 4 → 6 = 2k → k = 3. Equivalently, 2k + 1 − (k + 1) = 5k − 5 − (2k + 1) → k = 3. (b) The terms are 4, 7, 10 and d = 3. Check: (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 — the middle term is indeed the average of the other two ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the correct equation (average property or equal differences), 1 for correct algebra/simplification, 1 for k = 3. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three terms 4, 7, 10 and d = 3, 1 for the average check (4 + 10)/2 = 7.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — working via either equation; ‘k = 3’; check any valid form.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) For three consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence the middle is the average of the outer two: 2(2k + 1) = (k + 1) + (5k − 5) → 4k + 2 = 6k − 4 → 6 = 2k → k = 3. Equivalently, 2k + 1 − (k + 1) = 5k − 5 − (2k + 1) → k = 3. (b) The terms are 4, 7, 10 and d = 3. Check: (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 — the middle term is indeed the average of the other two ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the correct equation (average property or equal differences), 1 for correct algebra/simplification, 1 for k = 3. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three terms 4, 7, 10 and d = 3, 1 for the average check (4 + 10)/2 = 7.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — working via either equation; ‘k = 3’; check any valid form.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) For three consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence the middle is the average of the outer two: 2(2k + 1) = (k + 1) + (5k − 5) → 4k + 2 = 6k − 4 → 6 = 2k → k = 3. Equivalently, 2k + 1 − (k + 1) = 5k − 5 − (2k + 1) → k = 3. (b) The terms are 4, 7, 10 and d = 3. Check: (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7 — the middle term is indeed the average of the other two ✓.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the correct equation (average property or equal differences), 1 for correct algebra/simplification, 1 for k = 3. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the three terms 4, 7, 10 and d = 3, 1 for the average check (4 + 10)/2 = 7.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — working via either equation; ‘k = 3’; check any valid form."},{"n":63,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Square tiles are used to build L-shaped borders. Figure 1 uses 3 tiles, figure 2 uses 8 tiles and figure 3 uses 15 tiles. Show that the number of tiles in figure n is quadratic, and find the rule for the number of tiles, Tₙ.\\n\\nb Use your rule to find the number of tiles in figure 10.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Square tiles are used to build L-shaped borders. Figure 1 uses 3 tiles, figure 2 uses 8 tiles and figure 3 uses 15 tiles. Show that the number of tiles in figure n is quadratic, and find the rule for the number of tiles, Tₙ."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Use your rule to find the number of tiles in figure 10."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Terms 3, 8, 15. First differences: 5, 7. Second difference: 2 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 2 ÷ 2 = 1 for n². Try Tₙ = n² + bn + c: T₁ = 1 + b + c = 3; T₂ = 4 + 2b + c = 8. Subtracting: 3 + b = 5 → b = 2, then c = 0. Rule: Tₙ = n² + 2n. (Check: T₃ = 9 + 6 = 15 ✓.) (b) T₁₀ = 10² + 2 × 10 = 100 + 20 = 120 tiles.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for the differences 5 and 7, 1 for the constant second difference 2 with the conclusion), 1 for the rule Tₙ = n² + 2n (any equivalent working). (b) 2 marks: 1 for correct substitution into the candidate’s rule, 1 for 120.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘Tₙ = n² + 2n’; ‘constant second difference 2’; figure 10 = 120 for any consistent quadratic fitted to the three terms.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":63,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Differences of differences first, then build the rule.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) show the sequence is quadratic","prompt":"Show that the number of tiles in figure n is quadratic.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compute the gaps between consecutive terms.","Take the gap of the gaps — constant and non-zero means quadratic."],"accepted":["differences 5 and 7; second difference 2, constant — the sequence is quadratic","first differences 5, 7; second difference 2 (constant) ⇒ quadratic"],"keywords":[["5"],["7"],["2"],["second difference","second-difference"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) the rule Tₙ","prompt":"Find the rule for the number of tiles, Tₙ.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["The second difference fixes the n² coefficient — try a candidate with that leading coefficient and no constant term.","Fit b by comparing the first term of your candidate rule with 3."],"accepted":["Tₙ = n² + 2n","Tn = n^2 + 2n","n^2+2n","n² + 2n","T(n) = n^2 + 2n"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b) tiles in figure 10","prompt":"Use your rule to find the number of tiles in figure 10.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute n = 10 into your own rule from part (a).","Evaluate your rule at n = 10."],"answer":120,"tolerance":0,"unit":"tiles"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Terms 3, 8, 15. First differences: 5, 7. Second difference: 2 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 2 ÷ 2 = 1 for n². Try Tₙ = n² + bn + c: T₁ = 1 + b + c = 3; T₂ = 4 + 2b + c = 8. Subtracting: 3 + b = 5 → b = 2, then c = 0. Rule: Tₙ = n² + 2n. (Check: T₃ = 9 + 6 = 15 ✓.) (b) T₁₀ = 10² + 2 × 10 = 100 + 20 = 120 tiles.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for the differences 5 and 7, 1 for the constant second difference 2 with the conclusion), 1 for the rule Tₙ = n² + 2n (any equivalent working). (b) 2 marks: 1 for correct substitution into the candidate’s rule, 1 for 120.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘Tₙ = n² + 2n’; ‘constant second difference 2’; figure 10 = 120 for any consistent quadratic fitted to the three terms.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Terms 3, 8, 15. First differences: 5, 7. Second difference: 2 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 2 ÷ 2 = 1 for n². Try Tₙ = n² + bn + c: T₁ = 1 + b + c = 3; T₂ = 4 + 2b + c = 8. Subtracting: 3 + b = 5 → b = 2, then c = 0. Rule: Tₙ = n² + 2n. (Check: T₃ = 9 + 6 = 15 ✓.) (b) T₁₀ = 10² + 2 × 10 = 100 + 20 = 120 tiles.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for the differences 5 and 7, 1 for the constant second difference 2 with the conclusion), 1 for the rule Tₙ = n² + 2n (any equivalent working). (b) 2 marks: 1 for correct substitution into the candidate’s rule, 1 for 120.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘Tₙ = n² + 2n’; ‘constant second difference 2’; figure 10 = 120 for any consistent quadratic fitted to the three terms.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Terms 3, 8, 15. First differences: 5, 7. Second difference: 2 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 2 ÷ 2 = 1 for n². Try Tₙ = n² + bn + c: T₁ = 1 + b + c = 3; T₂ = 4 + 2b + c = 8. Subtracting: 3 + b = 5 → b = 2, then c = 0. Rule: Tₙ = n² + 2n. (Check: T₃ = 9 + 6 = 15 ✓.) (b) T₁₀ = 10² + 2 × 10 = 100 + 20 = 120 tiles.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for the differences 5 and 7, 1 for the constant second difference 2 with the conclusion), 1 for the rule Tₙ = n² + 2n (any equivalent working). (b) 2 marks: 1 for correct substitution into the candidate’s rule, 1 for 120.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘Tₙ = n² + 2n’; ‘constant second difference 2’; figure 10 = 120 for any consistent quadratic fitted to the three terms."},{"n":64,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A ball dropped from a tower has height h = 45 − 5t² metres after t seconds. Copy and complete the table of heights for t = 0, 1, 2, 3.\\n\\nb Show that the heights form a quadratic sequence, find the constant second difference, and explain how it is related to the coefficient of t².","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ball dropped from a tower has height h = 45 − 5t² metres after t seconds. Copy and complete the table of heights for t = 0, 1, 2, 3."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Show that the heights form a quadratic sequence, find the constant second difference, and explain how it is related to the coefficient of t²."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"time t (s)"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"height h (m)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) h(0) = 45 − 0 = 45 m; h(1) = 45 − 5 = 40 m; h(2) = 45 − 20 = 25 m; h(3) = 45 − 45 = 0 m. (b) First differences: −5, −15, −25. Second differences: −10, −10 — constant, so the heights form a quadratic sequence. The second difference equals 2 × (the coefficient of t²): here the coefficient is −5 and 2 × (−5) = −10 ✓. (This is the general result for quadratic sequences an² + bn + c: the second difference is 2a.)\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for two correct heights, 1 for all four (45, 40, 25, 0). (b) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for first differences, 1 for the constant second difference −10 with the conclusion), 1 for the 2a relationship correctly explained.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2 × coefficient of t²’; ‘second difference = −10’; table completed in metres.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":64,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Fill the table, then take differences twice.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) the four heights","prompt":"Copy and complete the table of heights for t = 0, 1, 2, 3.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Substitute t = 0, 1, 2, 3 into h = 45 − 5t².","Evaluate h at each t in the table."],"accepted":["45, 40, 25, 0","45 m, 40 m, 25 m, 0 m","t=0 → 45; t=1 → 40; t=2 → 25; t=3 → 0"],"keywords":[["45"],["40"],["25"],["0"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) why quadratic","prompt":"Show that the heights form a quadratic sequence and find the constant second difference.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["First differences: 40 − 45 = −5; 25 − 40 = −15; 0 − 25 = −25.","Take the differences of the differences — constant means quadratic."],"accepted":["first differences −5, −15, −25; second difference −10, constant — quadratic","differences −5, −15, −25; second differences −10 (constant), so quadratic"],"keywords":[["-5","−5"],["-15","−15"],["-25","−25"],["-10","−10","10"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) link to the t² coefficient","prompt":"Explain how the second difference is related to the coefficient of t².","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Second difference = 2 × (coefficient of t²).","The second difference equals twice the leading coefficient of t² — substitute and check."],"accepted":["the second difference equals twice the coefficient of t²: 2 × (−5) = −10, which matches","second difference = 2a where a is the coefficient of t²; here a = −5 so 2a = −10 ✓"],"keywords":[["twice","2 times","2×","2 x","double"],["-5","−5","five"],["-10","−10"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) h(0) = 45 − 0 = 45 m; h(1) = 45 − 5 = 40 m; h(2) = 45 − 20 = 25 m; h(3) = 45 − 45 = 0 m. (b) First differences: −5, −15, −25. Second differences: −10, −10 — constant, so the heights form a quadratic sequence. The second difference equals 2 × (the coefficient of t²): here the coefficient is −5 and 2 × (−5) = −10 ✓. (This is the general result for quadratic sequences an² + bn + c: the second difference is 2a.)\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for two correct heights, 1 for all four (45, 40, 25, 0). (b) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for first differences, 1 for the constant second difference −10 with the conclusion), 1 for the 2a relationship correctly explained.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2 × coefficient of t²’; ‘second difference = −10’; table completed in metres.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) h(0) = 45 − 0 = 45 m; h(1) = 45 − 5 = 40 m; h(2) = 45 − 20 = 25 m; h(3) = 45 − 45 = 0 m. (b) First differences: −5, −15, −25. Second differences: −10, −10 — constant, so the heights form a quadratic sequence. The second difference equals 2 × (the coefficient of t²): here the coefficient is −5 and 2 × (−5) = −10 ✓. (This is the general result for quadratic sequences an² + bn + c: the second difference is 2a.)\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for two correct heights, 1 for all four (45, 40, 25, 0). (b) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for first differences, 1 for the constant second difference −10 with the conclusion), 1 for the 2a relationship correctly explained.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2 × coefficient of t²’; ‘second difference = −10’; table completed in metres.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) h(0) = 45 − 0 = 45 m; h(1) = 45 − 5 = 40 m; h(2) = 45 − 20 = 25 m; h(3) = 45 − 45 = 0 m. (b) First differences: −5, −15, −25. Second differences: −10, −10 — constant, so the heights form a quadratic sequence. The second difference equals 2 × (the coefficient of t²): here the coefficient is −5 and 2 × (−5) = −10 ✓. (This is the general result for quadratic sequences an² + bn + c: the second difference is 2a.)\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for two correct heights, 1 for all four (45, 40, 25, 0). (b) 3 marks: 2 for showing quadratic (1 for first differences, 1 for the constant second difference −10 with the conclusion), 1 for the 2a relationship correctly explained.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘2 × coefficient of t²’; ‘second difference = −10’; table completed in metres."},{"n":65,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The first four terms of a quadratic sequence are 7, 14, 25, 40. Find its nth term in the form an² + bn + c. Show how you find a, b and c.\\n\\nb Use your rule to find the 10th term, and check it by continuing the difference pattern twice more.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first four terms of a quadratic sequence are 7, 14, 25, 40. Find its nth term in the form an² + bn + c. Show how you find a, b and c."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Use your rule to find the 10th term, and check it by continuing the difference pattern twice more."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Terms 7, 14, 25, 40. First differences: 7, 11, 15. Second difference: 4 ⇒ 2a = 4, a = 2. Subtract 2n² (2, 8, 18, 32) from the terms: 5, 6, 7, 8 — arithmetic with difference 1, i.e. n + 4. So uₙ = 2n² + n + 4. Check: n = 1 → 2 + 1 + 4 = 7 ✓; n = 4 → 32 + 4 + 4 = 40 ✓. (b) u₁₀ = 2 × 100 + 10 + 4 = 214. Check by differences: after 15 comes 15 + 4 = 19 (u₅ = 40 + 19 = 59; rule: 2×25 + 5 + 4 = 59 ✓) and then 23 (u₆ = 59 + 23 = 82; rule: 2×36 + 6 + 4 = 82 ✓). The pattern is consistent; continuing the check (next would be 27, 31, …) also lands on 214 for u₁₀.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the differences and second difference (4), 1 for a = 2 with a valid method, 1 for uₙ = 2n² + n + 4 (or a correct substitution system for a, b, c). (b) 2 marks: 1 for u₁₀ = 214, 1 for the difference-pattern check (19 and 23 giving 59 and 82).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — solving the three simultaneous equations a + b + c = 7, 4a + 2b + c = 14, 9a + 3b + c = 25 correctly gives the same rule and full marks.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":65,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Second difference first, then peel off 2n².","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) the nth term an² + bn + c","prompt":"Find the nth term of the quadratic sequence 7, 14, 25, 40 in the form an² + bn + c. Show how you find a, b and c.","kind":"expression","points":3,"hints":["Differences: 7, 11, 15; second difference 4 ⇒ 2a = 4, a = 2.","Subtract 2n² from each term; the remainder is arithmetic — read off b and c."],"accepted":["uₙ = 2n² + n + 4","un = 2n^2 + n + 4","2n^2+n+4","2n² + n + 4","a = 2, b = 1, c = 4","2n2 + n + 4"]},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) 10th term","promp