 2n² + n + 4. Check: n = 1 → 2 + 1 + 4 = 7 ✓; n = 4 → 32 + 4 + 4 = 40 ✓. (b) u₁₀ = 2 × 100 + 10 + 4 = 214. Check by differences: after 15 comes 15 + 4 = 19 (u₅ = 40 + 19 = 59; rule: 2×25 + 5 + 4 = 59 ✓) and then 23 (u₆ = 59 + 23 = 82; rule: 2×36 + 6 + 4 = 82 ✓). The pattern is consistent; continuing the check (next would be 27, 31, …) also lands on 214 for u₁₀.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the differences and second difference (4), 1 for a = 2 with a valid method, 1 for uₙ = 2n² + n + 4 (or a correct substitution system for a, b, c). (b) 2 marks: 1 for u₁₀ = 214, 1 for the difference-pattern check (19 and 23 giving 59 and 82).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — solving the three simultaneous equations a + b + c = 7, 4a + 2b + c = 14, 9a + 3b + c = 25 correctly gives the same rule and full marks.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Terms 7, 14, 25, 40. First differences: 7, 11, 15. Second difference: 4 ⇒ 2a = 4, a = 2. Subtract 2n² (2, 8, 18, 32) from the terms: 5, 6, 7, 8 — arithmetic with difference 1, i.e. n + 4. So uₙ = 2n² + n + 4. Check: n = 1 → 2 + 1 + 4 = 7 ✓; n = 4 → 32 + 4 + 4 = 40 ✓. (b) u₁₀ = 2 × 100 + 10 + 4 = 214. Check by differences: after 15 comes 15 + 4 = 19 (u₅ = 40 + 19 = 59; rule: 2×25 + 5 + 4 = 59 ✓) and then 23 (u₆ = 59 + 23 = 82; rule: 2×36 + 6 + 4 = 82 ✓). The pattern is consistent; continuing the check (next would be 27, 31, …) also lands on 214 for u₁₀.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for the differences and second difference (4), 1 for a = 2 with a valid method, 1 for uₙ = 2n² + n + 4 (or a correct substitution system for a, b, c). (b) 2 marks: 1 for u₁₀ = 214, 1 for the difference-pattern check (19 and 23 giving 59 and 82).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — solving the three simultaneous equations a + b + c = 7, 4a + 2b + c = 14, 9a + 3b + c = 25 correctly gives the same rule and full marks."}]'::jsonb) as x
